faq:
  - q: Does a learning difficulty mean low intelligence?
    a: No. A learning difficulty does not affect intelligence. A child may have the same cognitive capacity as their peers or a higher one, with the difficulty concentrated in specific areas such as reading, writing and spelling. On the learning difficulty affecting reading, the NHS states plainly that it "does not affect intelligence".
  - q: My child writes letters backwards. Is that a sign of a learning difficulty?
    a: 'Not on its own. Reversing letters is common in children who are learning to read and write, and in most it resolves by itself. What makes it worth an assessment is the pattern: reversals continuing past the second year of school, reading that stays slow and effortful, spelling that varies wildly, and progress staying slow despite real effort — all together.'
  - q: What is the difference between a learning difficulty and ADHD?
    a: 'They are different pictures, though they often occur together. In a learning difficulty the struggle is tied to a specific academic area — reading, writing or maths. In ADHD it gathers in attention, impulse control and organisation, and is not specific to a lesson. The article on [ADHD symptoms](/en/blog/what-is-adhd-symptoms-in-children.html) covers the distinction.'
  - q: Is my child lazy, or is there a learning difficulty?
    a: 'The distinction is drawn by looking at effort. In what gets called laziness, a child avoids trying and makes progress when they do try. In a learning difficulty a child gets stuck at the same place even when they try; there is a constant gap between the effort spent and the result. Over time that gap lowers motivation too — so the "laziness" is usually the outcome, not the cause.'
  - q: How is a learning difficulty assessed?
    a: An assessment does not rest on a single test. Developmental and school history, interviews with parents and teachers, samples of the child's reading and writing, and a structured cognitive assessment are all considered together. Having vision and hearing checked by a doctor is the first step in every case.
  - q: Do you carry out learning difficulty assessments at your centre?
    a: We carry out [neuropsychological assessment](/en/services/neuropsychological-assessment.html), and learning difficulty is one of its four reasons for referral. The process is completed with a written report, followed by a verbal feedback session. We can look at which step fits your child in a first conversation.
