blocks:
  - type: metin
    baslik: What is a learning difficulty?
    metin: |-
      A learning difficulty is a persistent struggle in a particular academic area that cannot be explained by a child's general cognitive capacity. The most common form affects reading; there are also forms that gather in handwriting and spelling, or in number and calculation. Which area the struggle sits in is what decides where support should start.

      None of these comes from poor teaching, lack of interest, or level of intelligence. They are linked to differences in how the brain maps language onto sound, automates symbols and uses working memory. The NHS describes the learning difficulty affecting reading as one "where your brain works differently to most people", and notes separately that it does not affect intelligence ([NHS, Dyslexia in children](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/dyslexia/)).
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Why "they could do it if they tried" is misleading
    metin: A child with a learning difficulty usually works **harder** than their peers for less result. That gap between effort and outcome wears motivation down over the years, until the child genuinely does stop trying. The reluctance you see at that point is not the cause of the picture — it is **the result of it.** Which is exactly why making the distinction early helps most.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: How the signs look at each age
    giris: 'The same difficulty appears in a different costume at every age. Broadly:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Preschool
        metin: Vocabulary widening slowly, difficulty with rhymes and word games, not hearing the syllables inside words, late learning of colour, shape and letter names, trouble holding sequences such as the days of the week or counting.
      - baslik: Years 1–2
        metin: Difficulty linking letters to sounds, a delay in getting reading at all, confusing similar letters (b/d, m/n), guessing at words instead of reading them, spelling the same word two different ways on one page.
      - baslik: Years 3–5
        metin: Slow, hesitant reading aloud, difficulty understanding what was read, messy written work full of spelling errors, homework taking far longer than it does for peers, avoiding reading out loud.
      - baslik: Secondary school
        metin: Difficulty taking notes, avoiding long texts, spoken work being markedly better than written work, running out of time in exams, and a drift towards disliking school and towards physical complaints such as stomach aches.
      - baslik: Adolescence
        metin: Academic avoidance and a low academic self-image, with "I can't do it anyway" settling in. At this age the picture usually looks less like a learning problem and more like a motivation or behaviour problem.
  - type: kart-grid
    baslik: Which areas are affected?
    giris: 'A learning difficulty affects not one skill but several linked ones:'
    kolon: 3
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Reading
        maddeler:
          - Letter-to-sound mapping
          - Reading speed and fluency
          - Recognising words
          - Understanding what was read
          - Avoiding reading aloud
      - baslik: Writing
        maddeler:
          - Inconsistent spelling
          - Letter formation and layout
          - Getting a sentence onto paper
          - Punctuation and capitals
          - Writing being very slow
      - baslik: Number and order
        maddeler:
          - Matching number to symbol
          - Following the steps of a calculation
          - Holding times tables
          - Time and clock concepts
          - Confusing sequence and direction
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: Vision and hearing are ruled out first
    metin: The most commonly missed cause of difficulty with reading and writing is a problem with vision or hearing. Both need to be checked by a doctor before an assessment begins. This step resolves quickly and can change the entire picture. A learning difficulty is a heading discussed **after other causes have been ruled out.**
  - type: metin
    baslik: What is it confused with?
    metin: |-
      A child struggling at school may be struggling for more than one reason, and these reasons frequently occur together.

      **Attention and impulse control.** In ADHD the difficulty is not lesson-specific; attention, organisation and waiting are affected everywhere. The article on [ADHD symptoms](/en/blog/what-is-adhd-symptoms-in-children.html) has the detail.

      **Anxiety.** An anxious child cannot write down what they know in an exam, avoids reading aloud, and slows down to avoid mistakes. The picture resembles a learning difficulty but the cause is different — the article on [anxiety in children](/en/blog/anxiety-and-separation-anxiety-in-children.html) covers that distinction.

      **Language and speech development.** A delay in language development affects reading directly and may go unnoticed until school age.

      **The language of instruction not being the home language.** This is common in North Cyprus. Where school is taught in a different language from the one spoken at home, a delay in reading can be explained away for a long time as "still settling into the language". Growing up bilingual does not cause a learning difficulty — but it can hide a real one for months.
  - type: numarali-liste
    baslik: How to proceed if you suspect something
    giris: 'The order matters. These five steps shorten the time spent wondering:'
    maddeler:
      - Have vision and hearing assessed. This is the first step in every case, and it resolves quickly.
      - Talk to the teacher. What is seen in the classroom, which tasks your child gets stuck on and what has helped is the most valuable input an assessment can have.
      - Collect samples. Keep examples from your child's exercise books, homework and exam papers. They are the most concrete record of how things change over time.
      - Ask for a structured assessment. When developmental history, interviews and cognitive assessment are read together, the area the difficulty comes from becomes visible.
      - Start support while you wait. Reading practice, adjustments in class and a settled routine at home do not need to wait for a conclusion.
    kapanis: None of these steps settles the outcome in advance. The aim is to see early what your child actually needs.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: What helps at home and at school
    giris: 'There are things to do while an assessment is still running. All of them lighten your child''s daily load now:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: Shorter reading, more often
        aciklama: Short, regular repetition works better than one long, effortful session. Fifteen minutes of reading a day stays with a child more than an hour once a week.
      - baslik: Do not let reading become a punishment
        aciklama: For a struggling child, reading aloud feels like a test. Taking turns, a parent reading a paragraph and the child continuing, or using audiobooks shares the load.
      - baslik: Ask for adjustments that reduce the writing load
        aciklama: Extra time, fewer questions, an oral option and help with note-taking are adjustments that can be made in the classroom. You can discuss these with a teacher without waiting for an assessment result.
      - baslik: Keep the area of strength visible
        aciklama: Most children with a learning difficulty are markedly good at something — speaking, thinking visually, working with their hands, sport. Keeping that area present in daily life is the most practical way to protect academic self-image.
      - baslik: Recognise effort separately from result
        aciklama: '"How did you study for this" focuses on the part your child can control, where "what did you get" does not. Over time that distinction protects motivation.'
      - baslik: Share the load as a parent
        aciklama: Homework hours are among the most common flashpoints in a family. You do not have to carry that alone; [parent counselling](/en/blog/parent-counselling.html) sessions can be held without your child coming in.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: Where to start in Famagusta
    metin: We carry out [neuropsychological assessment](/en/services/neuropsychological-assessment.html) at our centre, and learning difficulty is one of its four reasons for referral. The process is completed with a written report and then a verbal feedback session. Where the struggle comes with anxiety or school refusal, [child psychology](/en/services/child-psychology.html) sessions can run alongside it. The article on [neuropsychological assessment](/en/blog/neuropsychological-assessment.html) sets out how the process works step by step. If you would like to talk, we are here.
